      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "perineal suturing is a common event which affects women across a variety of international settings. Women have expressed pain related to perineal trauma in the postnatal period but little is known about pain during suturing. In addition, there is a lack of evidence to identify how professional decisions are made about pain management during the suturing process."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "to explore women and clinicians' response to pain during the suturing of second degree tears and episiotomies."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "mixed method feasibility study which included observation, questionnaires and interviews."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "a Hospital Trust in Northwest England."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
            "content": "40 women and 21 clinicians participated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS AND FINDINGS",
            "content": "mild, moderate and severe pain was measured via the McGill Pain Questionnaire-Short Form (MCPQ-SF). Psychological distress was identified via the Hospital Anxiety and Depression Scale (HADS). Semi-structured face to face interviews identified three themes, Psychological distress and future functioning; Variation in practice and Style of communication."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "KEY CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "women who experienced psychological distress during previous or current childbirth scored higher on HADS and MCPQ-SF, and appeared to express more concerns about future functioning and healing. Variation in practice exists and style of communication had the potential to make the difference for women."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
            "Label": "IMPLICATIONS FOR PRACTICE AND RESEARCH",
            "content": "the process of suturing is complex and is not a standalone event for a woman. It is crucial that health professionals consider previous and subsequent experience of perineal suturing. Future research aims to develop a decision tree to support pain management during suturing."
          }
        ]
      },
